                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "d27d5321-ec66-3f7b-aa22-c52177a3c189",
                        "entry_id": "a5fefe14-c444-3cfc-98ce-cc6f4e94e77f",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A king who has sovereignty over inferior kings or ruling princes; a ruler of an overkingdom; a king that is truly superior or supreme; an emperor."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "9d390ba5-5a69-4a72-becc-bfd09bd9e52d",
                                "sense_id": "d27d5321-ec66-3f7b-aa22-c52177a3c189",
                                "text": "the King of Connaught, who was recognized as overking of the island by the rest of the tribe[…]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0bef25a5-3498-4801-93e1-a043c2d0daa6",
                                "sense_id": "d27d5321-ec66-3f7b-aa22-c52177a3c189",
                                "text": "\"They have given me five-and-twenty overkings,\" protested John, and he at once turned to seek a way of evasion."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0ddbb51c-37bd-4c92-96ab-932412c8b424",
                                "sense_id": "d27d5321-ec66-3f7b-aa22-c52177a3c189",
                                "text": "The latter asked the aid of Cond the overking, who took up their cause, and a fierce war arose, in which Cond was beaten and compelled to divide Ireland with his rival."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "abf7de31-a5fe-4023-90ab-33ca7868625a",
                                "sense_id": "d27d5321-ec66-3f7b-aa22-c52177a3c189",
                                "text": "Celtic law, however, explicitly specified that any member of a tribe, including overkings/overqueens of provinces and Druid high priests/priestesses, could be stripped of their legal rights if they failed to execute the legal obligations of their stations."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "2774c3d5-9fdb-4e7a-ac61-e2924eda932d",
                                "sense_id": "d27d5321-ec66-3f7b-aa22-c52177a3c189",
                                "text": "The difference between a dux bellorum and an overking is not clear, and it may be that the two concepts were elided into a single role."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
